00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:00.990
JENNY BROWN: What do we want?

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:01.560
Data.

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.180
And where do we want it?

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:04.440
In buckets.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.690
In this episode of
Cloud Storage Bytes,

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.340
we take a look at
buckets and why

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.070
they are the cornerstone
of everything you do.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:12.210
Stay tuned for more.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:19.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:21.860
Before you can do
anything in Cloud Storage,

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.480
you've got to create a bucket.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.190
Because anything
you want to store

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:28.040
in Cloud Storage needs a
bucket in order for you

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:29.750
to do anything with it.

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:31.520
When configuring
your bucket, you

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.890
need to make three
initial decisions--

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:37.280
name, location,
and storage class.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.200
First, a bucket needs
a globally unique name,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.820
and this can't be changed.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.170
So choose a name that will be
relevant and useful to you.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.270
We've got great
documentation on naming,

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:50.220
so we'll go ahead and link
that in the description.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.690
Once you've named your bucket,
you need to select a location.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.120
You should choose location
based upon what type

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:59.870
of redundancy options you need,
where your primary users are,

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.030
and what your expected
first time to byte

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.519
is when caching is turned off.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:06.380
Generally, you should
store your data

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:09.320
in a location that's convenient
or contains the majority

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.280
of the users of your data.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.730
There are three different
types of locations--

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:17.060
region, dual-region,
and multi-region.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:18.800
This gives you
plenty of flexibility

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.740
in choosing the location
that will work best for you,

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.000
since location can't be changed
once that bucket is created.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.090
Use a region to help
optimize latency and network

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:32.570
bandwidth for data consumers,
such as analytics pipelines

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.730
that are grouped
in the same region.

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:37.820
Use a dual-region when you want
similar performance advantages

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:41.120
as regions, but you also want
the higher availability that

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.520
comes with being geo-redundant.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.260
And use a multi-region
when you want

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.810
to serve content to data
consumers that are outside

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:49.700
of the Google network
and distributed

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.340
across large geographic
areas, or when

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:55.940
you want the higher availability
that comes with geo-redundancy.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.400
And finally, you
choose a storage class,

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.200
which you can update
later on, but if you

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.830
don't select anything initially,
this will default to Standard.

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:07.120
Let's dive in and see
what that actually means.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.210
Cloud Storage has four
different storage glasses.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:12.970
All offer low latency
and high durability,

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:15.100
but they vary based
on their availability

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.130
and minimum storage durations,
along with the pricing

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.380
for storage and access.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:22.360
Data that will be
served at a high rate

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.930
with high availability should
use the standard storage class.

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:28.690
This class provides the best
availability with the trade

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.410
off of a slightly higher price.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:33.480
Data that will be
infrequently accessed

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.970
and can tolerate a
slightly lower availability

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.100
should be stored using
the nearline storage,

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.340
coldline storage, or
archive storage classes.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.230
Your choices here are
going to vary depending

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:45.190
on your specific needs.

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:47.640
I like to think about using
nearline for something I'll

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.090
access once a month or so,
and archive for something

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:53.580
I'll need about once
a year, and coldline

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:55.320
for the stuff in between.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.530
The documentation will help
you make the best choice here.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.810
Now that you know what
goes into Cloud Storage

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.240
and where it goes, we'll
get to the good stuff,

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.730
like how to get it there
and what to do with it.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.363
Until then, subscribe,
